fix(servers): the connect flow really is the one caller its guardrail claims to be

`connect-flow.ts`'s header and `servers/CLAUDE.md` both say it is the ONE caller of `connectSavedPlace` and of the
reconnect manager's lazy start, and that it picks the move by a volume's standing. Two of its three arms had no
production caller: `place-connect.svelte.ts` returns early unless the standing is `saved`, so only arm 3 was reachable,
while `smb-view-state.svelte.ts` called `smbReconnectManager.startCycle` and `openSignInForPlace` directly. A guardrail
doc describing a shape the code doesn't have is worse than no doc: the next person adds a fourth caller on its word.

Both pane paths now go through the flow.

- The lazy-nav landing on an already-`Disconnected` share calls `connectPlace({ connectionState: 'disconnected' })`,
  whose arm 1 is that `startCycle` and nothing else. The manager is idempotent, so landing on the same share twice
  still costs nothing.
- The signed-out banner's Sign in calls `connectPlace({ connectionState: 'needs_sign_in' })`, whose arm 2 supplies both
  `registered: true` (the volume is filed under this id, so the sheet mends it rather than dialing a second one into
  existence) and the `needs_credentials` reason the sheet's first round now says out loud. That is a real gain for the
  user, not just for the doc: the banner is up because a credential is missing, and the sheet used to open silent about
  it.

The manager's `needs-auth` IS the volume's `needs_sign_in`: that event is what flips the status.

`network/DETAILS.md`'s lazy-nav paragraph and `servers/DETAILS.md`'s three-arms section now name the three callers,
one per arm. New cell: the banner's Sign in reaching the sheet with its reason. The two existing `startCycle` cells
pass unchanged, which is the proof arm 1 does the same thing.
